    {
      "text": "Figure 8: Comparison of the trajectories in the Double Integrator Environment of an agent controlled by policies",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        317.12078857421875,
        503.99786376953125,
        325.1367492675781
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "obtained by RCRL (in red) and our proposed algorithm RESPO (in green) when starting from the infeasible set",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        327.082763671875,
        503.9992370605469,
        335.0987243652344
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(but still within the safe set). Notice how our approach actively enters the feasible set (blue region), while RCRL",
      "bbox": [
        107.7040023803711,
        337.0457763671875,
        504.4401550292969,
        345.0617370605469
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "fails to do so. The level set demarcating the feasible/infeasible set boundary is in black. The safe set (i.e. the set",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        347.0087890625,
        504.0006103515625,
        355.0247497558594
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "of states that have no violations) is the region within the dashed purple square. The infeasible set is in yellow.",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        356.97076416015625,
        500.5130615234375,
        364.9867248535156
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "We use the Double Integrator environment as a motivating example to demonstrate how performing",
      "bbox": [
        107.53199768066406,
        380.1263427734375,
        504.00360107421875,
        389.03289794921875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "constrained optimization using solely reachability-based value functions as in RCRL can produce",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        390.9158020019531,
        504.0028991699219,
        399.9419250488281
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "nonoptimal behavior when the agent is outside the feasiblity set. Double Integrator has a 2 dimensional",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        401.9443664550781,
        504.00140380859375,
        410.8509216308594
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "observation space [x1, x2], 1 dimension action space a ∈[−0.5, 0.5], system dynamics is ˙s = [x2, a],",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        412.13604736328125,
        505.24481201171875,
        422.4559020996094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "and constraint as ||s||∞≤5. Particularly, we make the cost as 1 if ||s||∞> 5, and 0 otherwise to",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        423.0450439453125,
        504.00030517578125,
        433.3648986816406
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "emphasize the importance of capturing the frequency of violation during training.",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        434.6713562011719,
        432.9599609375,
        443.5779113769531
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "We train an RCRL controller and RESPO controller in this environment, and the results are visualized",
      "bbox": [
        107.53199768066406,
        450.9407958984375,
        504.0036926269531,
        459.9669189453125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "in Figure 8. The color scheme indicates the learned reachability value across the state space while the",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        461.9693603515625,
        504.0015869140625,
        470.87591552734375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "black line demarcates the border of the zero level set. We present the behavior of the trajectories of",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        472.87835693359375,
        504.001220703125,
        481.784912109375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "RCRL and RESPO. Because the RCRL optimizes for reachability value function when outside the",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        483.66778564453125,
        504.0030212402344,
        492.69390869140625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "feasible set, it simply minimizes the maximum violation, which as can be seen does not result in the",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        494.69635009765625,
        504.0041198730469,
        503.6029052734375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "agent reentering the feasible set since it is uniformly equal to or near 1 in the infeasible set. This is",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        505.4459533691406,
        504.0003967285156,
        514.5119018554688
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "since it permits many violations of magnitude same or less than that of the maximum violation. On",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        516.514404296875,
        504.0025329589844,
        525.4209594726562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "the other hand, RESPO optimizes for cumulative damage by considering total sum of costs, thereby",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        527.3037719726562,
        504.3428039550781,
        536.3298950195312
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "re-entering the feasible set.",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        538.3333740234375,
        215.89498901367188,
        547.2399291992188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
